chi1 = -y*cos(t*w) + x*sin(t*w)
chi2 = (m*w*x*cos(t*w) + m*w*y*sin(t*w) - py*cos(t*w) + px*sin(t*w))/m
chi3 = (m*w^2*y*cos(t*w) - m*w^2*x*sin(t*w) + 2*px*w*cos(t*w) + 2*py*w*sin(t*w) - lam)/m
chi4 = -(m*w^3*x*cos(t*w) + m*w^3*y*sin(t*w) - 3*py*w^2*cos(t*w) + 3*px*w^2*sin(t*w) + u)/m
chi2 - (n.p/m + w e.x) = 0
lambda - (2 w e.p - m w^2 n.x) = 0  -> lambda = 2 w p_r on the rod: the Coriolis force
u from chi4 = 0: u == -m*w^3*x*cos(t*w) - m*w^3*y*sin(t*w) + 3*py*w^2*cos(t*w) - 3*px*w^2*sin(t*w)
d/dt r - p_r/m = w*y*cos(t*w) - w*x*sin(t*w)  (= -w n.x, zero on the rod)
d/dt p_r + w n.p = 0  and n.p = -m w r on the surface, so d/dt p_r = m w^2 r: r'' = w^2 r
